If you throw an error in the `catch` block, the error will propagate up the call stack, and you can catch it in another part of your code and handle it appropriately. However, the return type of the `fetchUser` function will still be `Promise<User | null>`, as the function is still expected to either return a `User` object or `null`.

If you want to change the return type of the function to `Promise<User | Error>`, you would need to modify the implementation of the function to return an `Error` object instead of `null` in case of failure. For example:
```typescript
async function fetchUser(userId: number): Promise<User | Error> {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${userId}`);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Failed to fetch user with status code ${response.status}`);
    }
    const user = await response.json();
    return user;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`An error occurred while fetching the user: ${error}`);
    return error;
  }
}

interface User {
  id: number;
  username: string;
  email: string;
}
```

A promise in Node.js is an object that represents the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation, and its resulting value. Promises are a way to handle asynchronous operations in a more organized and manageable way than using callbacks.

Here's an example of using a promise to read a file from the filesystem:
```
const fs = require('fs');

const readFile = fileName => {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fs.readFile(fileName, 'utf8', (err, data) => {
      if (err) {
        reject(err);
      } else {
        resolve(data);
      }
    });
  });
};

readFile('example.txt')
  .then(data => {
    console.log(data);
  })
  .catch(err => {
    console.error(err);
  });
```
In this example, `readFile()` returns a new promise that wraps the `fs.readFile()` function. The promise is resolved with the contents of the file if the read is successful, or rejected with an error if it fails. The `then()` and `catch()` methods are used to handle the resolved value or rejected reason, respectively.

